{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And Leah went out, etc.</b> The reason she acted thus and not in accordance with proper conduct is according to what I explained regarding the word ״לָכֵן״ that Rachel said — that it means ״עַל כֵּן״ (therefore): Leah was afraid that Rachel might retract, and therefore she went ahead and went out. And according to what I explained that ״לָכֵן״ is an oath: since Rachel did not specify to say ״this night,״ Leah was afraid she might put her off to another night — and in this she was zealous and gained." "<b>Further,</b> one may give a reason for Leah's conduct, for she acted in the way of piety: she did not leave him be until that righteous man would enter Rachel's tent and then take him out of there, for that would involve insistence and great distress for Rachel and intense jealousy. Therefore she went ahead of him before he would enter her tent — and this is nothing but a good and pious trait, and the entire intent of that righteous woman was to establish the plantings of Na'aman (i.e., to raise up righteous offspring). And HaShem is witness to the matter, for He gave her her reward, as it is written ״וַיִּשְׁמַע״ etc. (Genesis 30:17). And see what I explained nearby (other texts: what Rashi explained) on this verse." "<b>I have hired you, etc.</b> The meaning is: I gave payment to another on your account. It further intends that she hired two things: lodging and conjugal connection." "<b>It is for the night.</b> The meaning is — with his will and consent, that he agreed to the matter."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {1 "The phrase נִטְעֵי נַעֲמָן ('plantings of Na'aman') is a fixed expression from Numbers 24:6 used idiomatically to mean noble offspring; rendered literally here."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:45:07.096193451Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}